INT. SYNERGEX CORP - OFFICE OF THE VICE PRESIDENT OF CULTURE - DAY

A glass-walled corner office that looks onto a parking structure. A yoga ball sits where a chair should. A neon sign on the wall reads "BE THE CHANGE (PENDING APPROVAL)." A whiteboard is covered with arrows that lead nowhere. Sunlight is filtered through a fake ficus.

VICE PRESIDENT BARRETT QUIGLEY-STONE, 40s, sleeves rolled, wireless headset on one ear, bounces gently on the yoga ball. His hair is gelled into a decision. Across the desk sits PRUDENCE OKONJO, 30s, a quality analyst, pressed blazer, notebook on her knee, patience worn thin. In front of her is a paper cup of kombucha she has not touched.

BARRETT
Prudence. Thanks for jumping on this.

PRUDENCE
You asked me to come at nine. It's nine-forty.

BARRETT
Time is a social construct. But I hear you. Let's circle back to your feedback.

PRUDENCE
It's not feedback. It's a defect report. The product ships with a battery that overheats.

BARRETT
We prefer "enthusiastic."

PRUDENCE
It caught fire in testing.

BARRETT
Enthusiastic. Let's workshop that.

Barrett stands, bounces once on the ball, and caps a marker. He writes ENTHUSIASM on the whiteboard and draws an arrow to a drawing of a flame. Prudence watches without blinking.

PRUDENCE
Barrett, I filed this report six weeks ago.

BARRETT
And we love that. Proactive. Self-starter. That's the energy.

PRUDENCE
It's still unanswered.

BARRETT
Which is why I brought you here. I want to empower you to own the narrative.

PRUDENCE
I don't want to own the narrative. I want to stop the shipment.

BARRETT
Shipment is a strong word.

PRUDENCE
Forty thousand units are in a warehouse in Ohio. Trucks leave Friday.

BARRETT
(leaning in, smiling)
Let's not boil the ocean.

Prudence puts the notebook on the desk. She picks up the kombucha and sets it down again, harder than she meant to. Liquid slops over the rim.

PRUDENCE
Do you know what a thermal runaway is?

BARRETT
Sounds like a marathon.

PRUDENCE
It's when a battery cell heats the next one and the next. It doesn't stop. It's a chain reaction in someone's pocket.

BARRETT
And that's why we're building a culture of openness.

PRUDENCE
You're not hearing me.

BARRETT
I'm hearing the concern. I'm holding space for it.

Prudence stands. Her chair rolls backward and hits the ficus. A leaf falls off. It is plastic.

PRUDENCE
People will get burned.

BARRETT
Prudence. Breathe.

PRUDENCE
Don't tell me to breathe.

BARRETT
We have a mindfulness app. It's free for staff.

Prudence takes the marker out of his hand. She crosses out ENTHUSIASM and writes FIRE in letters a foot tall. The marker squeaks. Barrett's smile flickers, then holds.

BARRETT (CONT'D)
That's not on brand.

PRUDENCE
Neither is a lawsuit.

BARRETT
Now you're being negative.

PRUDENCE
I'm being accurate.

Barrett slides off the yoga ball. It rolls away and bumps a wall. He picks up his headset, taps it.

BARRETT
(into headset)
Hey, Dale. Quick favor, can you hold the trucks?

He listens. His face drops.

BARRETT (CONT'D)
(into headset)
What do you mean, they left Tuesday?

Prudence stares at him. Barrett slowly removes the headset and places it on the desk like a dead bird.

PRUDENCE
Tuesday.

BARRETT
There may have been a miscommunication.

PRUDENCE
You called me in here to talk me out of it. The trucks were already gone.

BARRETT
I was going to loop you in.

PRUDENCE
You were going to loop me out.

She picks up her notebook. Barrett stands very still. The neon sign hums. Prudence flips to a fresh page, writes something, tears it out, and sets it on his desk.

BARRETT
What is this?

PRUDENCE
My resignation. And the number for the fire marshal.

She walks out. The door swings behind her. Barrett stares at the page. He sits on the floor where the yoga ball used to be, and the neon sign flickers off.
